WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.520
NARRATOR:
Heading back to shore,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.320
the team review the footage

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.040
from cameras mounted
on the bait lines.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.360
MEGAN: Chh chh chh chh chh.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.280
Oh, that's such a good one!

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.080
HEATHER: So that's when the buoy
went down. You on to that, Meg?

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.400
GREG: Look at that.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.200
MEGAN: Oh, it really wanted
that thing. This is awesome.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.480
GREG: Oh, it tears it up.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.480
NARRATOR:
With no claspers visible,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.920
the shark is a juvenile female

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.880
and new to the Atlantic
white shark database.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.440
MEGAN: I think we put
this one at like nine. Right?

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:38.880
HEATHER: I've, yeah,
I think so, too.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.000
MEGAN: A nine or ten-footer,
so not a super big one,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.760
still a big shark, but not
really big for a white shark.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.040
GREG: You think this is
typical size

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.760
you guys are seeing up here?

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.000
HEATHER: It seems to be
a lot of the juveniles,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.160
a lot of the acoustic detections
we get, it's, it's in that,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.280
you know, let's say
9 to 12-foot range.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.040
It's, it's the smaller guys.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.680
GREG: Yeah.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.800
NARRATOR:
It's an important clue

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.360
and different to the pattern
Greg and Megan see

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.600
some 260 miles to the south
around Cape Cod.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.320
GREG: We do see juveniles,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.560
but a lot of our resident sharks
are big males.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.080
And those big males may be
schoolyard bullies, you know,

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.320
pushing these smaller animals
into other parts of their range,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.480
which include, in the summer
and the fall, Canadian waters.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.200
NARRATOR: In 2019, a drone
operator captured sharks

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.360
clashing off Cape Cod.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.680
Could territorial adult males

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.080
be pushing smaller
white sharks north?

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.280
GREG: We also know there are
social interactions

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.840
between these sharks
because we see scars.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.760
You know, there are bites,
there are injuries

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.200
that are clearly
from other sharks.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.320
And is that associated
with mating?

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.520
Certainly it could be.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.680
But it, on juveniles,
it could also be associated

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.400
with negative interactions
between sharks.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.160
In other words,
"Get the hell out of here.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.680
This is my neighborhood."

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.480
♪ 

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.520
NARRATOR: Territory may
just be part of the puzzle

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.160
of what's drawing sharks north.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.480
Canada's Atlantic waters
have some of the richest

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.880
fishing grounds in the world.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.600
Sharks in the eight to
nine-feet range are youngsters

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.720
and, for the most part,
fish-hunters.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.720
The shape of the seabed here,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.960
combined with the cold
Labrador Current

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.320
as it mixes with
the Gulf Stream,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.840
brings nutrients
up to the surface.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.320
(birds squawking)

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.960
It makes for the perfect
conditions for marine life

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.720
to flourish
in spectacular numbers.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.120
♪ 

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.720
Canada is also experiencing

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.280
one of its hottest
Augusts on record.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.800
Could the mix of so much food
and warming waters

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.880
be part of what draws
white sharks north?

